</style><div class="page"><header class="masthead"><p class="eyebrow">Fortress revival &middot; 2026-11-24 &middot; the record&rsquo;s consolidation, rounds 1 and 2</p><h1>Consolidation pilot</h1><p class="standfirst">The coordinator&rsquo;s page of the pilot. Each entry of the sample was rewritten twice, by the batch mode&rsquo;s workflow and by hand. This page sets the two beside the old text, with where each claim went, so that the curator can judge whether the consolidation works on each kind of entry, makes the record smaller and clear, and loses nothing.</p></header><section id="what" class="part"><h2><span class="num">1</span>What this page shows</h2>
<p>The curator ordered a consolidation of the whole of FACTS: 339 entries, 427K characters. Each entry is to hold one subject; a claim that another entry owns becomes a pointer to that entry; history, plans, a defect&rsquo;s description and detail move to their homes. Before the whole record is rewritten, the pilot shows the method on a sample in two rounds: round 1, one case of each kind that the sorting found, and round 2, the 30 entries of one chunk, on inference. The pilot was written two ways: by the batch mode&rsquo;s workflow on its first run, and by hand, by one Opus writer for each round who followed the sorting map&rsquo;s disposition for each entry. Each way&rsquo;s result was then checked by fresh readers and a checker.</p>
<p><strong>How to read an item.</strong> An item is one entry. It opens with the entry&rsquo;s number and subject, its sizes, and what the map says to do with it. Then come the new text by hand, whole; the new text by the workflow, whole, or a line that says the workflow kept the entry; how the two differ; where each claim that left the entry now stands; and where the hand writer did not follow the map, with the reason. The old text is last, folded under &ldquo;Old text&rdquo;. In round 2, an entry that the hand writer merged into another stands inside its owner&rsquo;s item.</p>
<dl class="gloss">
<dt>claim</dt><dd>one thing that an entry says, which can be true or false on its own</dd>
<dt>sorting map</dt><dd>list, made before the pilot, of every claim of FACTS by subject and kind, with a disposition for each entry</dd>
<dt>disposition</dt><dd>what the map says to do with an entry: keep it as it is, rewrite it in place, split it, merge it into another, or take it out of FACTS</dd>
<dt>owner</dt><dd>entry that states a subject best today, and so takes the claims on that subject from other entries</dd>
<dt>split</dt><dd>move of an entry&rsquo;s claims on other subjects to the owners of those subjects</dd>
<dt>merge</dt><dd>removal of an entry whose claims then stand whole in its owner</dd>
<dt>history</dt><dd>file beside FACTS that keeps each old line word for word, with what moved where</dd>
<dt>report</dt><dd>note of a piece of work that holds its detail, such as lines of code and measurements; an entry names its section</dd>
<dt>row</dt><dd>numbered line of the gap ledger, the list of known gaps; a row&rsquo;s notes hold a defect&rsquo;s description</dd>
<dt>chunk</dt><dd>set of 20 to 32 entries on related subjects that one writer rewrites in the full run</dd>
<dt>assessor</dt><dd>first agent of the workflow, which marks each entry keep, reword, reorganize or investigate; only reword and reorganize reach the workflow&rsquo;s writer</dd>
<dt>cold reader</dt><dd>reader that sees a new entry with no other context and says what it means</dd>
<dt>checker</dt><dd>agent that compares each new entry with its old text and its sources</dd>
<dt>judge</dt><dd>last agent of the workflow, which applies or holds each rewritten entry</dd>
<dt>count stage and distance stage</dt><dd>two stages of the gate that run the compiled checker over the interpreter&rsquo;s library and count its errors</dd>
</dl></section><section id="numbers" class="part"><h2><span class="num">2</span>The numbers</h2>
<p>The sorting map read the 339 entries and listed 2,652 claims. By kind: 1,213 properties of the tree; 422 pointers to other entries; 387 defects; 220 lists of test names; 208 details that belong in a report; 140 history; 33 choices that a worker made; 29 plans.</p>
<p>It sorts the entries so: 182 stay as one entry, 21 of them as they are and 161 rewritten in place; 119 are split, into 2.3 parts on average; 30 are merged into another entry; 8 are not about the tree. In 205 entries a defect&rsquo;s description goes to its row. Five subjects have no owner and need a new entry. The full run cuts FACTS into 12 chunks of 25 to 31 entries, and 53 parts, 64 claims in all, move from one chunk to another.</p>
<div class="tbl" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Sizes of the pilot"><table><caption>Entries and characters before and after, for each round and each way. Characters are those of each entry&rsquo;s line in FACTS.</caption><thead><tr><th>Round and way</th><th>Entries</th><th class="n">Characters</th><th class="n">After, of before</th><th>Characters waiting for other chunks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Round 1, by hand</th><td class="nw">19 to 19</td><td class="n">20,472 to 18,351</td><td class="n">90 %</td><td>none in its entries</td></tr><tr><th>Round 1, by the workflow</th><td class="nw">17 to 17</td><td class="n">18,788 to 13,630</td><td class="n">73 %</td><td>not marked</td></tr><tr><th>Round 2, by hand</th><td class="nw">30 to 23</td><td class="n">37,978 to 31,032</td><td class="n">82 %</td><td>1,309 out, 674 in</td></tr><tr><th>Round 2, by the workflow</th><td class="nw">30 to 26</td><td class="n">37,978 to 30,884</td><td class="n">81 %</td><td>not marked</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<ul>
<li>Round 1&rsquo;s workflow run had the 17 entries that the cases name. The hand round also rewrote F-171 (the specification's reading of <code>comprises</code>) and F-98 (the harness's check keys), since each takes a claim from a case. On the same 19 entries, the workflow&rsquo;s result is 15,314 characters, 75 % of before.</li>
<li>The workflow rewrote 10 of its 17 entries in round 1, and 13 of the 30 in round 2, with 4 merges. Its assessor kept the others as they are.</li>
<li>Round 1 by hand moved no claim that must wait. Two moves into its entries wait for the chunk &ldquo;export&rdquo;: three claims of F-282 (the export check of the one library) for F-286 (<code>String</code>'s abstract members in the api), and the merge of F-289 (the distance stage's setting) into F-111 (what can move the count and distance stages). The workflow left both undone too.</li>
<li>Round 2 by hand: 1,309 characters are claims that the map sends to entries of other chunks. They stay here, marked, until those chunks run. 674 characters came in from entries of other chunks, which keep them until their own chunk runs. Without both, the chunk would hold about 29,000 characters, 77 % of before.</li>
<li>The workflow marks no claim as waiting. A claim that the map sends out of its chunk either stays where it is or leaves the entry for the history (section 6).</li>
</ul><div class="refs"><p class="refs-title">References</p><p>Paths below are under <code>explorations/coordinator/postmortem-2026-11-24-record-regrowth/</code>. "Hand" is <code>evidence/pilot-2/</code>; "workflow" is <code>evidence/pilot-workflow/chunks/</code>; "map" is <code>evidence/sorting-map/MAP.md</code>.</p><ol><li>The sorting: map, sections 1 and 2.</li><li>Hand sizes: Hand <code>round-1.md</code>, "The sizes"; <code>round-2.md</code>, the head and "Sizes, before and after". Recounted from <code>round-1.apply</code> and <code>round-2.apply</code> against FACTS at the base <code>92f2e628d4</code>; they agree.</li><li>Workflow sizes: counted for this page from workflow <code>pilot-1/final.apply</code> and <code>inference/final.apply</code> against FACTS at the same base, the line with its leading "- ", as <code>record.py</code> counts it. FACTS is the same at the workflow&rsquo;s base <code>2d1614aaee</code> and on <code>main</code>.</li><li>The moves between chunks: Hand <code>round-2.md</code>, "Moves between chunks"; Hand <code>round-1.md</code>, "Where the map could not be followed"; map, section 5, the lines of F-282 and F-289.</li></ol></div></section><section id="round1" class="part"><h2><span class="num">3</span>Round 1, case by case</h2>
<p>Round 1 is a mix: one case of each kind that the sorting found. The map names seven cases. Case 2, a merge of two entries, lies in the chunk on inference, so section 4 shows it. The six cases here hold 19 entries by hand and 17 for the workflow. Each case is an item; its entries are items inside it.</p>
<div class="item case" id="case-1"><h3 class="ih">Case 1. A split</h3><p><strong>Its kind.</strong> An entry with several subjects. F-374 (a compiled object's <code>var</code> field) holds the cell of a <code>var</code> field, which it owns, and also how an atomic expression is compiled, which F-351 (compiled transactions) owns.</p><p><strong>What the sorting said to do.</strong> F-374 keeps the cell. Its part on the atomic expression, two claims, goes to F-351. "(row 319, fixed)" goes to the history, and the one library's <code>Lazy</code> to the report. The descriptions of three defects go to rows 641, 1043 and 112.</p><div class="item entry" id="F-374"><h4 class="ih">F-374, a compiled object's <code>var</code> field</h4><p class="sizes">Old 1,770 characters. By hand 1,236. By the workflow 1,019.</p><p class="map"><span class="maplab">The map:</span> split: the atomic expression to F-351; one claim to the history and one to the report; three defects to their rows.</p><p class="lab hand">By hand</p><div class="newtext hand">F-374 <strong>A compiled object's <code>var</code> field lives in a cell that the object holds, and every read and write of it takes the code of a top-level <code>var</code>.</strong> On the compiled path a <code>var</code> value parameter or <code>var</code> field of an object is a <code>MutableFValue</code> cell in a <code>public final</code> JVM field, made by the constructor. Each read and write checks <code>BaseTask.inATransaction()</code> and goes through the transaction or the cell, and a loop body or a function expression in the object's method captures the cell. So an <code>atomic</code> block tracks a <code>var</code> field, and every access of one pays the transaction check, outside <code>atomic</code> too (row 641: the check's measured cost). A field without <code>var</code> holds its value and has no check. The batch's planner chose the cell as a default, and the curator has not decided it. A change of the transaction's variable code reaches fields with no second edit, and a new kind of nested context must capture a <code>var</code> field's cell as it does a top-level <code>var</code>'s. In a <code>value</code> object, a <code>var</code> field, which nothing refuses, makes two objects built alike not strictly equivalent (row 112). Gated by <code>compiler_tests/AtomicFieldCompiled</code>. Detail: <code>compile-ladder/rung-atomic-expr-field/REPORT.md</code>, "What changed and why" and "The bytecode".</div><p class="lab wf">By the workflow</p><div class="newtext wf">F-374 <strong>On the compiled path, an object's <code>var</code> field is a <code>MutableFValue</code> cell that the object holds, read and written by the same emitted code as a top-level <code>var</code>.</strong> A <code>var</code> value parameter is one too. So an <code>atomic</code> block tracks the field, and a change to that code reaches these fields with no second edit. Each access checks for a transaction, outside <code>atomic</code> too (row 641: the check's cost). A field without <code>var</code> has no check. A loop body or function expression in a method captures the cell; a new kind of nested context must capture it too, as it does a top-level <code>var</code>'s. A batch's planner chose the cell as a default, not the curator. A <code>value</code> object with a <code>var</code> field, which the specification forbids and nothing refuses (row 112), is not strictly equivalent (<code>===</code>) to another built alike, since <code>SEQV</code> compares the cells by identity; the library's <code>Lazy</code> is one. Gated by <code>compiler_tests/AtomicFieldCompiled</code> and <code>VarFieldCapturedCompiled</code>. Detail: <code>compile-ladder/rung-atomic-expr-field/REPORT.md</code>.</div><p class="lab">How the two differ</p><p>Both ways move the atomic expression to F-351, and keep the cell, its capture and the cost of its transaction check. The hand text sends the <code>value</code> object's example, <code>V(1) === V(1)</code> with <code>SEQV</code>, to row 112, which holds it, and names one test. The workflow keeps <code>===</code>, <code>SEQV</code> and <code>Lazy</code> in the entry, names two tests, and keeps the example only in the history.</p><p class="lab">Where the claims that left it went, by hand</p><ul class="moves"><li>How <code>atomic e</code> is compiled, with the block's limits (row 322): F-351.</li><li>A numeral as the atomic expression's value where a type is declared (row 1043): F-351.</li><li>"(row 319, fixed)": the history. The tracking itself stays.</li><li>The measured cost of the transaction check: row 641.</li><li><code>V(1) === V(1)</code> is <code>false</code>, since <code>SEQV</code> compares the cells; the specification forbids such a field: row 112.</li><li>The one library's one such object, <code>Lazy</code>: row 112's notes.</li><li>Two of the three tests: the report, which names all three.</li></ul><p class="lab">Where the hand writer did not follow the map</p><p>The map sends the claim that an <code>atomic</code> block tracks a <code>var</code> field to the history. The writer kept it: only "(row 319, fixed)" is history, and the tracking is a present property that a test gates. The map sends <code>Lazy</code> to the report, which does not name it, so it went to row 112, which does.</p><details class="old"><summary>Old text, 1,770 characters</summary><div>F-374 <strong>A compiled object's <code>var</code> field lives in a cell that the object holds, and an atomic expression is its body in an atomic block.</strong> On the compiled path a <code>var</code> value parameter or <code>var</code> field of an object is a <code>MutableFValue</code> cell in a <code>public final</code> JVM field, made by the constructor; each read and write checks <code>BaseTask.inATransaction()</code> and goes through the transaction or the cell, by the code of a top-level <code>var</code>, and a loop body or a function expression in the object's method captures the cell. The batch's planner chose the cell as a default, and the curator has not decided it. So an <code>atomic</code> block tracks a <code>var</code> field (row 319, fixed), and every access of one pays the transaction check, outside <code>atomic</code> too (row 641). A field without <code>var</code> holds its value and has no check. <code>atomic e</code> is compiled by <code>CodeGen.forAtomicBlock</code> on the block that holds <code>e</code> (row 875, fixed), with the block's limits (row 322); a numeral as its value at a place of declared type is refused or fails verification, since the checker carries no declared type into it (row 1043). So a change of the transaction's variable code reaches fields with no second edit, and a new kind of nested context must capture a var field's cell as it does a top-level <code>var</code>'s. A <code>value</code> object with a <code>var</code> field, which the specification forbids and nothing refuses (row 112), is not strictly equivalent to another built alike: <code>V(1) === V(1)</code> is <code>false</code>, since <code>SEQV</code> compares the cells by identity. The one library declares one, <code>Lazy</code> (<code>Library/Lazy.fss:18</code>). Tests: <code>compiler_tests/AtomicFieldCompiled</code>, <code>compiler_tests/VarFieldCapturedCompiled</code>, <code>compiler_tests/AtomicExprCompiled</code>. Detail: <code>compile-ladder/rung-atomic-expr-field/REPORT.md</code>, "What changed and why" and "The bytecode".</div></details></div><div class="item entry" id="F-351"><h4 class="ih">F-351, compiled transactions</h4><p class="sizes">Old 1,900 characters. By hand 2,186. By the workflow 1,486.</p><p class="map"><span class="maplab">The map:</span> split: it keeps its subject, which it owns; its order of exception-table entries goes to F-373 (label and exit on the compiled path); its source lines go out.</p><p class="lab hand">By hand</p><div class="newtext hand">F-351 <strong>A compiled <code>atomic</code> block runs as a transaction that validates itself and every ancestor, and an abort restarts the block whose transaction it names; an atomic expression is compiled as such a block.</strong> The transaction records no read of its own write; it records a read of an ancestor's write with that write, a read of the committed value with that value, and a read of an empty array element with <code>EMPTY</code>, a mark that validation reads as no value. The global lock is a counter that each commit raises, and a transaction keeps a value of it, its snapshot, set when it starts and at each validation. Validation, at a read once the counter has moved past the snapshot and at a commit that finds it moved, covers the transaction and every ancestor. An abort names the transaction whose read failed; <code>abort()</code> names none. The block's handler takes every exception (<code>BaseTask.leaveTransaction</code>): it ends the block's transaction, starts the block again for an abort that names that transaction or names none (or any abort at the outermost block), commits the transaction on an exit, a <code>LabelExit</code>, and throws the exit on, or takes the abort path where that commit fails (F-401), and throws everything else on, after a nested transaction has handed its reads to its parent. A nested commit hands its parent its reads and its writes, also where it wrote nothing. The exception-table entry of a block or a <code>try</code> follows its body's code, so a nested one is taken first. A nested block is not atomic against a plain write that another thread of its outer block makes (row 984). The code generator compiles <code>atomic e</code>, for a body other than a <code>do</code> block, as the atomic block that holds <code>e</code> alone, so the expression has the block's limits (row 322: as a call's later argument it fails verification). The checker carries no declared type into an atomic expression's body, so a numeral as its value where a type is declared is refused or fails verification (row 1043). Gated by <code>compiler_tests/*Atomic*Compiled</code>. Detail: <code>compile-ladder/rung-compiled-transaction/REPORT.md</code>, "What changed and why"; <code>compile-ladder/rung-atomic-expr-field/REPORT.md</code>, "What changed and why" (the expression).</div><p class="lab wf">By the workflow</p><div class="newtext wf">F-351 <strong>A compiled <code>atomic</code> block's transaction validates itself and every ancestor, and an abort restarts the block whose transaction it names.</strong> A transaction records each read with what it read: an ancestor's write, the committed value, or a mark for an empty array element; never a read of its own write. It validates when a read finds the global lock moved, and at a commit that finds the lock moved since its snapshot. An abort names the transaction whose read failed, or none from <code>abort()</code>. The block's handler restarts the block on an abort that names its transaction or none, and on any abort at the outermost block. It commits on an exit and throws the exit on, or runs the block again where that commit fails (F-401: exits out of atomic blocks). It throws any other exception on, after a nested transaction hands its parent its reads. A nested commit hands the parent its reads and writes, even if it wrote nothing. Open: a nested block is not isolated from a write that another arm of its outer block makes outside any nested block (row 984). <code>atomic e</code> compiles as an atomic block around <code>e</code>, with that block's limits (row 322: it fails verification as a call's later argument). The checker carries no declared type into <code>e</code>, so where the context declares a type, a numeral as <code>e</code>'s value is refused or fails verification (row 1043). Gated by <code>compiler_tests/Atomic*Compiled</code> and <code>NestedAtomicAbortCompiled</code>. Detail: <code>compile-ladder/rung-compiled-transaction/REPORT.md</code>.</div><p class="lab">How the two differ</p><p>Both take the atomic expression from F-374. The hand text defines the global lock, the snapshot and the empty element's mark, keeps the handler in one long sentence, and keeps the order of exception-table entries. The workflow splits the handler into four sentences and leaves the lock and the mark undefined. It took the order out for F-373, which it could not rewrite, so that sentence now stands only in the history.</p><p class="lab">Where the claims that left it went, by hand</p><ul class="moves"><li>The source lines: the history. The report gives them at its own commit.</li><li>Nine test names: one name pattern, which matches seven of them. The report names all nine.</li></ul><p class="lab">Where the hand writer did not follow the map</p><p>The map sends the order of exception-table entries (a block's or a <code>try</code>'s entry follows its body, so a nested one is taken first) to F-373. The writer kept it here: this entry's rule for a nested abort rests on it, and it came from this entry's report.</p><details class="old"><summary>Old text, 1,900 characters</summary><div>F-351 <strong>A compiled transaction validates itself and every ancestor, and an abort restarts the block whose transaction it names.</strong> A compiled <code>atomic</code> block's transaction records no read of its own write; it records a read of an ancestor's write with that write, a read of the committed value with that value, and a read of an empty array element with a mark. Validation, at a read once the global lock has moved and at a commit that did not get the lock at its snapshot, covers the transaction and every ancestor. An abort names the transaction whose read failed; <code>abort()</code> names none. The block's handler takes every exception (<code>BaseTask.leaveTransaction</code>): it ends the block's transaction, starts the block again for an abort that names that transaction or names none (or any abort at the outermost block), commits the transaction on an exit, a <code>LabelExit</code>, and throws the exit on, or takes the abort path where that commit fails (F-401), and throws everything else on, after a nested transaction has handed its reads to its parent. A nested commit hands its parent its reads and its writes, also where it wrote nothing. The exception-table entry of a block or a <code>try</code> follows its body's code, so a nested one is taken first. Open: a nested block sees a plain write by another thread that runs its outer block (row 984). Source: <code>ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/runtimeSystem/Transaction.java:228-367</code>, <code>runtimeSystem/BaseTask.java:295-322</code>,<code>compiler/codegen/CodeGen.java:2148-2180</code> (<code>forAtomicBlock</code>), <code>:2490-2494</code> (<code>forTry</code>). Tests: <code>compiler_tests/AtomicOwnWriteNestedCompiled</code>, <code>AtomicNestedSameBodyCompiled</code>, <code>NestedAtomicAbortCompiled</code>, <code>AtomicNestedViewCompiled</code>, <code>AtomicSharedReadsCompiled</code>, <code>AtomicThrowRevertsCompiled</code>, <code>AtomicNestedReadsKeptCompiled</code>, <code>NestedTryInnerCatch</code>; <code>ArrayStoreJUTest</code>. Detail: <code>compile-ladder/rung-compiled-transaction/REPORT.md</code>, "What changed and why".</div></details></div></div><div class="item case" id="case-3"><h3 class="ih">Case 3. A defect beside its property</h3><p><strong>Its kind.</strong> An entry that states a property and also describes a defect. F-279 (walk's slices of arrays based above zero) says that walk slices an <code>Array2</code> or <code>Array3</code> based above zero like a 0-based one, and describes the defect of a shifted <code>Array2</code>.</p><p><strong>What the sorting said to do.</strong> The property stays. The defect's description goes to row 808, and the entry names the row in a few words. The source lines go to the report. The entry stays beside F-157 (the idiom for an array view), which owns array views and slices.</p><div class="item entry" id="F-279"><h4 class="ih">F-279, walk's slices of arrays based above zero</h4><p class="sizes">Old 766 characters. By hand 866. By the workflow 736.</p><p class="map"><span class="maplab">The map:</span> rewritten in place: its source lines to the report; its defect to row 808.</p><p class="lab hand">By hand</p><div class="newtext hand">F-279 <strong>Under walk an <code>Array2</code> or <code>Array3</code> based above zero slices like a 0-based one, except an <code>Array2</code> that <code>shift</code> moved to a new base.</strong> <code>Array2.subarray</code> and <code>Array3.subarray</code> hand their 0-based views the offset less the base, and <code>Array3</code>'s <code>[:]</code> passes its base, as <code>Array1</code>'s do. Such a shifted <code>Array2</code> is a <code>SubArray2</code> view, and <code>SubArray2</code>'s own <code>subarray</code> (<code>Library/FortressLibrary.fss:3083</code>) subtracts the output base instead of its own. So a slice of a shifted <code>Array2</code> reads the wrong elements, and <code>shift</code> of a slice, a column, a row or a shifted <code>Array2</code> ends in Java's <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code> (row 808: its cases, the one-line repair and a way around). Gated by <code>tests/Array*SliceAtNonzeroBase</code>; row 808's expected failure is <code>tests/XXXArray2ShiftedSlice</code>. Detail: <code>compile-ladder/rung-slices-list-equality/REPORT.md</code>, sections 1 and 5.</div><p class="lab wf">By the workflow</p><div class="newtext wf">F-279 <strong>Under walk, a slice of an <code>Array2</code> or <code>Array3</code> based above zero reads the right elements, except a slice of a shifted <code>Array2</code>; a shift of an <code>Array2</code>'s slice, column or row, or of a shifted <code>Array2</code>, throws.</strong> A shifted array is a view that <code>shift</code> made at another base. A slice or a shift of an <code>Array2</code> is a <code>SubArray2</code> view. <code>SubArray2</code>'s own <code>subarray</code>, which slices such a view again, subtracts the output's base instead of its own (row 808). So a slice of a shifted <code>Array2</code> reads the wrong elements, and those shifts end in <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>. Gated by <code>tests/Array2SliceAtNonzeroBase</code>, <code>Array3SliceAtNonzeroBase</code> and <code>XXXArray2ShiftedSlice</code>. Detail: <code>compile-ladder/rung-slices-list-equality/REPORT.md</code>.</div><p class="lab">How the two differ</p><p>Both name the defect's two failures and point to row 808. The hand text keeps why the other slices are right, keeps the defect's own source line, and says in a few words what the row holds. The workflow moves that mechanism and every line to the history, and names three tests where the hand text names one pattern and the defect's expected failure.</p><p class="lab">Where the claims that left it went, by hand</p><ul class="moves"><li>Four source lines: the history. The report gives the lines of its own day.</li><li>The defect's source line: stays until row 808 gives today's line (a new text for the row is proposed).</li><li>The wrong elements and the failure of each shift: row 808 holds both. The entry keeps them too, since the cold reader could not tell which shifts fail.</li><li>Three test names: one pattern, and the defect's expected failure by name.</li></ul><p class="lab">Where the hand writer did not follow the map</p><p>The map sends all the source lines to the report. The report gives older lines, so the defect's line stays in the entry until row 808 is updated.</p><details class="old"><summary>Old text, 766 characters</summary><div>F-279 <strong>Under walk an <code>Array2</code> or <code>Array3</code> based above zero slices like a 0-based one, except a shifted <code>Array2</code>.</strong> <code>Array2.subarray</code> and <code>Array3.subarray</code> hand their 0-based views the offset less the base, and <code>Array3</code>'s <code>[:]</code> passes its base, as <code>Array1</code>'s do (<code>Library/FortressLibrary.fss:2999</code>, <code>:3401</code>, <code>:3380</code>; <code>:2711</code>). <code>SubArray2</code>'s own <code>subarray</code> subtracts the output base instead of its own (<code>:3083</code>, row 808), so a slice of a shifted <code>Array2</code> still reads the wrong elements, and a shift of a slice, a column, a row or a shifted <code>Array2</code> ends in Java's <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>. Tests: <code>Array2SliceAtNonzeroBase</code>, <code>Array3SliceAtNonzeroBase</code>, <code>XXXArray2ShiftedSlice</code>. Detail: <code>compile-ladder/rung-slices-list-equality/REPORT.md</code>, sections 1 and 5.</div></details></div><div class="item entry" id="F-157"><h4 class="ih">F-157, the idiom for an array view</h4><p class="sizes">Old 1,117 characters. By hand 988. By the workflow 777.</p><p class="map"><span class="maplab">The map:</span> rewritten in place: "measured under walk only" to the history; its two measurements to rows 298 and 299.</p><p class="lab hand">By hand</p><div class="newtext hand">F-157 <strong>An array is a trait with <code>get</code> and <code>put</code>, and a view is an object that implements it over other storage; this is the language's own idiom for a view of a runtime shape, as the library's own <code>TransposedMatrix</code> is.</strong> Under walk, a subtype of <code>Matrix</code> that overrides <code>mul</code> gets its own product through the library's <code>Matrix</code> juxtaposition and <code>DOT</code>, which forward to <code>mul</code>. It needs no api line, since walk dispatches the method on the receiver's run-time type; the override was measured under walk only. Under walk the library's own views cost more than a user's: its row slice <code>m[i,:]</code> five times a six-line user view (row 298), and its product through a diagonal held as a plain <code>Matrix</code> view 13 times a row-scaling operator at n = 16, and 58 to 63 times at n = 64 (row 299, which says 54 at n = 64). Detail: <code>reviews/c4-flatarrays-review.md</code> section 1 (the idiom); <code>run-c4/probes/vocabulary/diag-override.md</code>, "Result" (the override) and "Timings against row 299" (the ratios).</div><p class="lab wf">By the workflow</p><div class="newtext wf">F-157 <strong>The language's idiom for a view of a runtime shape is an object that implements an array trait (<code>Array</code> or a subtrait such as <code>Matrix</code>), with its <code>get</code> and <code>put</code>, over other storage, as the library's <code>TransposedMatrix</code> does.</strong> Under walk, a subtype of <code>Matrix</code> that overrides <code>mul</code> gets its own product through the library's <code>Matrix</code> juxtaposition and <code>DOT</code>, which call <code>mul</code>. It needs no declaration of the override in any api, since walk dispatches on the run-time type; the compiled path was not measured. Two measurements under walk found library views slower than hand-written code: the row slice (row 298) and a diagonal as a <code>Matrix</code> view in a product (row 299). Detail: <code>reviews/c4-flatarrays-review.md</code> section 1; <code>run-c4/probes/vocabulary/diag-override.md</code>.</div><p class="lab">How the two differ</p><p>Both keep the idiom and the override of <code>mul</code> under walk. The hand text keeps the two measured ratios with their sizes and leaves the commit and the matrix's size to row 298. The workflow keeps only that two measurements found the library's views slower, and leaves every number to the rows and the history.</p><p class="lab">Where the claims that left it went, by hand</p><ul class="moves"><li>The commit and the 64 by 16 matrix of the first measurement: row 298.</li><li>Row 299's 54 against the probe's 58 to 63: kept, and a new text for row 299 is proposed.</li></ul><p class="lab">Where the hand writer did not follow the map</p><p>The map calls "measured under walk only" history. The writer kept it: it is the limit of the claim on the override.</p><details class="old"><summary>Old text, 1,117 characters</summary><div>F-157 <strong>An array is a trait with <code>get</code> and <code>put</code>, and a view is an object that implements it over other storage; this is the language's own idiom for a view of a runtime shape, as the library's own <code>TransposedMatrix</code> is.</strong> Under walk, a subtype of <code>Matrix</code> that overrides <code>mul</code> gets its own product through the library's <code>Matrix</code> juxtaposition and <code>DOT</code>, which forward to <code>mul</code>. It needs no api line, since walk dispatches the method on the receiver's run-time type; the override was measured under walk only. In two measurements under walk at <code>b176a4b79</code>, views through the library cost more. On lifting a function over the rows of a 64 × 16 matrix, the row slice <code>m[i,:]</code> took five times as long as a six-line user view (row 298). The library's product with a diagonal that is a plain <code>Matrix</code> view, without the override, took 13 times as long as a row-scaling operator at n = 16, and 58 to 63 times at n = 64 (row 299, which says 54). Detail: <code>reviews/c4-flatarrays-review.md</code> section 1 (the idiom); <code>run-c4/probes/vocabulary/diag-override.md</code>, "Result" (the override) and "Timings against row 299" (the ratios).</div></details></div></div><div class="item case" id="case-4"><h3 class="ih">Case 4. An entry that today's checks found false or stale</h3><p><strong>Its kind.</strong> An entry with claims that are false today, citations that are gone, and one claim whose ground is unsettled. F-63 (three lines of the one library's api) holds four statements on four lines of the one library's api. The hand writer found that nothing joined them but the work of one rung, a task of a batch, on one day.</p><p><strong>What the sorting said to do.</strong> F-63 stays beside F-286 (<code>String</code>'s abstract members in the api), the owner of the api's members against its components. Its claim on <code>String</code>'s <code>split</code> pair goes to F-286, without its false reason. Its three other statements stay, with rows 355 and 357 named. Its claim on <code>QQ</code> goes to F-64 (the checker's refusal at a <code>comprises</code> clause with <code>...</code>). Two claims go to the history. The unsettled ground is settled before any new wording.</p><div class="item entry" id="F-63"><h4 class="ih">F-63, three lines of the one library's api</h4><p class="sizes">Old 1,265 characters. By hand 1,036. By the workflow, unchanged.</p><p class="map"><span class="maplab">The map:</span> split: its claim on <code>QQ</code> to F-64; two claims of history out; checks found it false today and unsettled.</p><p class="lab hand">By hand</p><div class="newtext hand">F-63 <strong>Three lines of the one library's api follow their components, as the compiled export check asks: <code>List</code> declares no <code>zip</code> of its own, the sequential generator <code>Condition</code>'s <code>map</code> returns <code>Condition[\G\]</code>, and <code>QQ</code> is closed by <code>comprises { ... }</code>.</strong> <code>List</code> takes the <code>zip</code> that it inherits from <code>ZeroIndexed</code>, as its component does. <code>QQ</code>'s component lists its one value type, <code>Ratio</code>, which is private to the component, and an api may leave out a private type only by <code>...</code>. A worker chose each form (the report's "Decisions"). That the count and distance stages would refuse <code>Condition.map</code>'s earlier <code>SequentialGenerator[\G\]</code> is read from the export check's code (F-282), not measured. Two other apis still depart in the same ways: <code>Library/PureList.fsi</code> declares a <code>zip</code> that its component lacks (row 355), and <code>Library/IntMap.fsi</code>'s closed trait needs <code>...</code> for a private type, and the checker then refuses the extender that the api declares (row 357). Detail: <code>compile-ladder/rung-library-defects/REPORT.md</code>, "Decisions".</div><p c